g symmetry. The key insight may lie in the symmetry of the outputted 2s and the application of this across the grid. The symmetry occurs with respect to the center of the grid. Train 4 clearly demonstrates this: columns 0 & 5, and 2 & 3 show it with respect to 2. Is this symmetry the rule? I'm now testing.\n\n\n**Pinpointing Transformation Focus**\n\nI've made a breakthrough. I realized that 2s and 5s exclusively replace 8s in the transformation, while zeros remain untouched. I've now verified across Train examples that the count of 8s always matches the total of 5s and 2s in the output. My attention is now focused on discerning the conditions that determine whether an 8 becomes a 2 or a 5. I am zooming in on Train 1 for this.\n\n\n**Uncovering Symmetry Logic**\n\nI'm now deeply immersed in the spatial arrangement of the '2's, looking for a core geometric concept. I see reflections across the horizontal center line, where the '2's are symmetrically positioned across the central rows. I am making progress, and I am now going to validate this in Train 1, 2, 3, and 4. I feel like I'm close.\n\n\n**Validating Reflection Symmetry**\n\nI've just made a significant breakthrough! I've been testing a horizontal reflection rule on Train 1 and Train 2 to understand the placement of the '2's. If a cell with the value 8 has a reflection on the opposite side of the grid (across the horizontal center line) that is also an 8, it converts to a '2'. If the cell at the reflection is not 8, then the value converts to a 5. I'm now certain I'm on the right track!\n\n\n",
